# Line endings, decided here rather than by each machine's git configuration.
#
# **Written because a test went red in every fresh worktree on this machine and green in the
# main tree.** `core.autocrlf=true` converts on checkout, so `ops/install/install.sh` arrived
# with carriage returns in a clean checkout and `test_the_committed_script_has_unix_line
# endings_and_nothing_else` reported the file as broken when the committed bytes were right.
# That test is worth keeping exactly as it is: a shell script with carriage returns fails on a
# server with `bad interpreter`, which is a failure a client meets on their first install.
#
# So the shape of a file is a property of the repository. Anything a Linux server executes or
# reads is LF whatever the machine editing it prefers.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff2 binary
